WEBVTT

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.680
Prompts and outputs should be treated as potentially sensitive data.

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:07.445
They may contain user identifiers, private documents, source code, secrets,

00:00:07.445 --> 00:00:11.900
business plans, regulated content, customer records, or internal operational details.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:16.335
Even if the model service is private, the surrounding logs, analytics, debug traces,

00:00:16.335 --> 00:00:19.680
prompt history, and storage systems can become data exposure paths.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.320
Log minimization is a practical privacy control.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:25.970
Capture enough information to operate the service, investigate errors,

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:27.570
and support audit requirements,

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:31.720
but avoid storing full prompts and outputs unless there is a clear need and an approved

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:32.420
retention model.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:35.260
Redaction can help at a high level, but it is not a guarantee.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.910
Sensitive values may appear in unexpected forms,

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:41.230
and overly broad debug logging can defeat careful API design.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:45.445
Retention policies should define what is stored, where it is stored, who can access it,

00:00:45.445 --> 00:00:46.680
and when it is deleted.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:49.875
Prompt history may be valuable for user experience or troubleshooting,

00:00:49.875 --> 00:00:52.020
but it also creates privacy obligations.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:56.760
Local storage, cloud storage, backups, and analytics pipelines should all be considered.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.670
Data copied into secondary systems can outlive the original request unless deletion and

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:02.640
retention are designed intentionally.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:06.500
Privacy expectations should be communicated to application owners and users.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.020
If prompts are logged, say so.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.300
If outputs are retained for review, define who can review them.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:16.010
If data is sent to a hosted provider, understand provider terms and organizational policy.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.590
A well-run LLM API does not rely on vague assumptions about privacy.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.560
It defines data handling as part of the service contract.
